<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leptis Magna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leptis Magna was a major city on the coast of Tripolitania, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> and later a prosperous Roman city. It was linked by trade to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a>, which supplied goods such as wild animals for Roman arenas. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>, born in Leptis Magna, campaigned against the Garamantes in 202 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leptis-magna.jpg" alt="Leptis Magna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: joepyrek from Richmond, Va, USA · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leptis_Magna_(29)_(8288918733).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Leptis Magna stood at the mouth of the Wadi Lebda on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast. Its ruins lie within present-day Al-Khums, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, 130 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>. The Punic name was written lpq, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called it Leptis Megale, Greater Leptis, to tell it from the Lesser Leptis nearer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. As a Roman colony it was also known as Ulpia Traiana, after the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Punic city was founded in the second half of the 7th century BCE. It appears to have been strong enough to repel an attempt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorieus">Dorieus</a> to found a Greek colony nearby around 515 BCE. It came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and then, after the Punic Wars, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, but stayed highly independent after about 111 BCE. By 46 BCE its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> production allowed it to pay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> three million pounds of oil a year as tax. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> it was formally incorporated into the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>; it became a municipium in 64 or 65 CE and a colonia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>.</p><p>Leptis reached its highest prominence from 193 CE as the home town of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>, who built a new forum, a massive basilica and new docks, and visited with his family in 205. The city reached a population of about 100,000 and prospered through trans-Saharan trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, wild animals for the arena, gold dust, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrich-feather">ostrich feathers</a>. When trade collapsed in the Crisis of the 3rd Century its importance fell, and by the mid 4th century large parts were abandoned, before a tsunami in 365 devastated it.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> took Leptis in 439 and demolished its walls. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> raiders sacked it in 523. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> recaptured it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> ten years later, but it never recovered from Berber attacks. Around 647 it fell to the Arab conquest, when it was mostly abandoned except for a Byzantine garrison and fewer than 1,000 inhabitants. By the 10th century Al-Khums had absorbed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Abandoned, the city lay buried beneath sand dunes and was remarkably preserved. Italian archaeologists unearthed it in the 1920s, and one of the first buildings found, in 1925 and 1926, was the Arch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>. Its ruins are among the best-preserved Roman sites in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Mosaics of the 1st or 2nd century, including a gladiator resting beside his slain opponent, were found in a Roman villa at Wadi Lebda and are displayed in the Leptis Magna Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leptis Magna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hundreds of towns flourished, from Carthage and Hadrumetum to Thugga, Thuburbo Maius, Sufetula, Bulla Regia, Thysdrus and <b>Leptis Magna</b>, with forums, baths, theatres, amphitheatres and floor mosaics of outstanding quality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Zliten Mosaic near <b>Leptis Magna</b>, which appears to show captives, possibly Garamantes, thrown to wild beasts in the arena, reflects how closely Roman Tripolitania watched this frontier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ammianus Marcellinus describes their raids of 363 to 365, which devastated the farms around <b>Leptis</b> and Oea, and the failure of the military count Romanus, who demanded supplies and camels before acting.</blockquote><a href="/state/austuriani">Austuriani</a></li><li><blockquote>Its capital was <b>Leptis Magna</b>, and the province stretched from the Gulf of Gabès to the Gulf of Sirte.</blockquote><a href="/state/tripolitania">Tripolitania</a></li></ul></section>
